WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.160
Welcome to this detailed look inside the ORv3 frame.

2
00:00:07.940 --> 00:00:12.460
In this lesson, we'll identify the key physical specifications of

3
00:00:12.460 --> 00:00:17.520
Open Rack version 3, including its updated dimensions, the busbar

4
00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:20.940
system, and the power shelf that makes it so efficient

5
00:00:20.940 --> 00:00:22.260
for modern data centers.

6
00:00:23.120 --> 00:00:26.520
The ORv3 rack is the result of years of development

7
00:00:26.520 --> 00:00:31.420
work and collaboration between rack and component vendors, as well

8
00:00:31.420 --> 00:00:34.280
as the hyperscalers and wider OCP community.

9
00:00:35.240 --> 00:00:38.540
Over the last three years, this design has evolved to

10
00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:43.000
address the changing needs of high density, scalable data centers.

11
00:00:44.180 --> 00:00:45.900
Let's start at the front of the rack.

12
00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:49.820
One of the first physical features you'll notice is the

13
00:00:49.820 --> 00:00:51.440
new modular cable manager.

14
00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:56.300
This modular piece can be unscrewed and removed to allow

15
00:00:56.300 --> 00:00:59.580
for customized cable management across the entire aisle.

16
00:01:01.040 --> 00:01:05.080
Modular cable management gives operators the flexibility to organize

17
00:01:05.080 --> 00:01:10.100
cables neatly and adjust layouts as needs change, which helps

18
00:01:10.100 --> 00:01:13.200
with airflow and maintenance in large -scale deployments.

19
00:01:14.140 --> 00:01:19.280
Inside the rack frame, ORv3 can now accommodate both OU

20
00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.120
and RU shelves from top to bottom.

21
00:01:23.480 --> 00:01:27.700
In ORv2, there was only Open U positioning, but now

22
00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:30.120
standard rack units are supported too.

23
00:01:31.060 --> 00:01:34.960
This expanded shelf support means that operators can install more

24
00:01:34.960 --> 00:01:39.220
equipment with standard sizing, making the rack more versatile for

25
00:01:39.220 --> 00:01:43.600
different IT gear. Another key update is that these shelves

26
00:01:43.600 --> 00:01:47.160
are designed to be tool -less, saving time during installation.

27
00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.080
Now let's move to the back of the rack.

28
00:01:51.120 --> 00:01:54.580
One of the biggest improvements here is the upgraded busbar

29
00:01:54.580 --> 00:01:59.400
power system. In the previous version, ORv2 racks used a

30
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:04.180
12v busbar, but ORv3 has stepped up to a 48v

31
00:02:04.180 --> 00:02:09.900
system. This upgrade to 48v delivers higher power efficiency and

32
00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:14.660
greater capacity, which is crucial for AI and HPC workloads

33
00:02:14.660 --> 00:02:16.700
that require denser power distribution.

34
00:02:18.300 --> 00:02:22.100
The busbar is fully integrated into the rack frame, with

35
00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:25.920
easy access for connecting power shelves and IT gear.

36
00:02:26.960 --> 00:02:29.780
It's one of the core features that sets Open Rack

37
00:02:29.780 --> 00:02:31.840
apart from traditional designs.

38
00:02:33.120 --> 00:02:38.420
In addition to the busbar, ORv3 racks include features that

39
00:02:38.420 --> 00:02:40.920
future -proof the frame for advanced cooling.

40
00:02:42.360 --> 00:02:46.000
The frame has dedicated spaces to support cold -arm cooling

41
00:02:46.000 --> 00:02:49.540
manifolds when liquid cooling becomes more widely adopted.

42
00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:54.500
This means that as AI servers push power rack requirements

43
00:02:54.500 --> 00:02:58.860
higher, operators can switch from air cooling to liquid cooling

44
00:02:58.860 --> 00:03:01.320
without replacing the entire rack structure.

45
00:03:02.700 --> 00:03:06.700
Structurally, the ORv3 frame is built to handle heavy loads.

46
00:03:07.940 --> 00:03:12.740
Each rack can now dynamically support up to 1400kg, which

47
00:03:12.740 --> 00:03:14.380
has been tested and approved.

48
00:03:15.680 --> 00:03:19.560
This is an increase in strength and stability compared to

49
00:03:19.560 --> 00:03:24.520
previous designs. Having a higher load capacity means more equipment

50
00:03:24.520 --> 00:03:28.480
can be installed without compromising stability or airflow.

51
00:03:29.480 --> 00:03:33.580
This is especially important for data centers that are constantly

52
00:03:33.580 --> 00:03:35.860
upgrading or retrofitting equipment.

53
00:03:37.020 --> 00:03:41.240
All these features combined make the ORv3 rack more flexible,

54
00:03:41.820 --> 00:03:45.480
powerful, and ready for the demands of next -generation workloads.

55
00:03:47.160 --> 00:03:50.900
From its improved cable management to the upgraded busbar and

56
00:03:50.900 --> 00:03:55.760
shelf system, every design choice supports efficiency and scalability.

